# The raw table layer is byte-exact generated output: every shard's sha256 is
# recorded in its table index, and `python -m tools.decode_all` must reproduce
# the working tree byte for byte on any platform. Git's autocrlf would rewrite
# line endings on checkout and break both. Never translate anything under it.
raw/tables/** -text

# Same reasoning, same guarantee, for the non-DBC raw layers. Each of these
# records sha256s that are checked against the bytes on disk - the Interface
# census (`bytesAt` -> sha256, gated by tests/test_raw_layers.py), the .loc and
# WDB shards, and the shard hashes in each index.json - and each extractor is
# gated on reproducing its output byte for byte. A CRLF rewrite on checkout
# would invalidate all of it at once, on Windows clones only, silently.
#
raw/content/** -text
raw/interface/** -text
raw/interface_all/** -text
raw/cache/** -text
raw/README.md -text

# The rest of the generated tree, added after the narrow scope above proved to
# be the wrong call. `raw/interface/**` was committed under core.autocrlf=true
# BEFORE it had an attribute here, so git stripped CR on add: 212 of the 1,456
# files the Interface census points `bytesAt` at no longer hashed to their
# recorded sha256, and the repo did not contain the client's bytes. Adding the
# attribute afterwards froze the damage in place instead of undoing it (the fix
# was to re-extract from the client with the attribute already in force).
#
# Every path below writes bytes some index records a sha256 for, so all of them
# were exposed to exactly that failure. Left un-attributed, `raw/_inventory` and
# `raw/realms` would have been next. Widening costs nothing: these files are
# generated LF-only, so with `-text` the index bytes and the working tree agree
# on every platform instead of only on Linux. `data/**` is the legacy curated
# tree; it is covered for the same reason, and its committed bytes are unchanged
# by the widening (they were already LF in the index - only the working-tree
# copy was being rewritten on checkout).
raw/_inventory/** -text
raw/dbc/** -text
raw/realms/** -text
raw/talents/** -text
raw/provenance.json -text
data/** -text

# The generated search catalog. tools/build_catalog.py must reproduce all four
# files byte for byte on any platform - tests/test_catalog.py regenerates them
# and compares sha256 - so a CRLF rewrite on checkout would fail the gate on
# Windows clones only, and silently.
raw/_catalog/** -text
CATALOG.md -text

# The binaries layer. Its shards, its recovered Lua chunks and its resource
# payloads are all bytes lifted verbatim out of a PE image, every one of them
# with a sha256 recorded in an index and re-checked against the client by
# tests/test_binaries.py. A CRLF rewrite on checkout would corrupt the recovered
# script and break every hash at once, on Windows clones only.
raw/binaries/** -text

# The recovery layer. Same reasoning again: every shard under it is generated
# LF-only and carries sha256/md5 values that are checked against bytes, and any
# recovered member bytes under it are stored verbatim - a CRLF rewrite on
# checkout would corrupt those outright.
raw/recovered/** -text
